    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "6.3 Residual subspaces and their annihilators"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 17,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 81.6, 523.929, 513.719, 558.021 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "As pointed out before, we are especially interested in D−invariant subspaces J with σ(D|J ) = ∅. Such subspaces will be called residual. They possess an alternative characterization based on the following simple observation."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 18,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 81.6, 468.136, 513.626, 515.781 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Lemma 3. If J ∈ J and p is a polynomial then p(D)J is closed. Proof. It suﬃces to show that (D − λI)J , λ ∈ C is closed. Let (xk) be a sequence in J such that (D − λI)xk → y ∈ H. Then there is a sequence (ck) in C such that"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 19,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 174.96, 448.096, 513.746, 467.314 ],
    "font" : "CMMI10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "V (I − λV )−1(D − λI)xk = xk − ckφλ → V (I − λV )−1y, (30)"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 20,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 81.6, 392.296, 513.609, 445.461 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "when k → ∞. If φλ ∈ J , it follows that (xk) converges to x ∈ J and y = (D − λI)x ∈ (D − λI)J . If φλ ∈/ J , let ϕ be a continuous linear functional in J ⊥ with ϕ(φλ) = 1. Then applying ϕ to both sides of (30) we obtain that (ck) converges and as above, we obtain that (xk) converges to x ∈ J and y = (D − λI)x ∈ (D − λI)J ."
  }, {
